  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 406 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nGusty southeast winds 30-40 mph within the Red River Valley will\ngradually decrease some after sunset. However, winds do stay \nelevated overnight, but not as strong as this afternoon. \nTemperatures also stay quite mild in the upper 50s into the 60s \novernight, well above average for this time of year. This is a\nresult of a deepening low pressure system in the northern High \nPlains. \n\nThis is also driving showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon into evening. Showers and weak thunderstorms will \ncontinue to migrate north and northeast, with relatively highest\nchance for lightning activity in the northern Red River Valley \ninto northwest MN. Very little rain amounts will result from \nthis, with those that see thunderstorms seeing at most half an \ninch.\n\nAs the low pressure system deepens, it propagates into south-\ncentral Canada, bringing with it a sweeping cold front still expected\nto move west to east late overnight tonight into Saturday \nmorning. Behind the front will be shifting and increasing winds\nout of the west. Confidence has increased enough to issue a Wind \nAdvisory for winds sustained near 30 mph and gusts to 50 mph in\nthe Devils Lake basin of northeast ND Saturday morning.\nSimilarly strengthen winds may also bleed into the far western\nedge of the Red River Valley. Winds are forecast to gradually\ndecrease starting early afternoon Saturday.\n\nTemperatures for Saturday will follow a non-diurnal curve, with\ndaily maximum temperatures likely to occur during predawn hours\nSaturday ahead of the front. With the front moving through\nSaturday morning, temperatures in the afternoon will be either\nsteady or falling.\n\nBy Saturday evening, temperatures turn seasonable with freezing\ntemperatures expected area wide Saturday night/Sunday morning,\nas well as during the overnight/early morning periods\nthroughout next week, perhaps thereafter as well.\n\nEnsemble guidance still shows potential for a hybrid-type,\nprogressive low pressure system to move through the Northern\nPlains and Upper Midwest next week around early to mid next\nweek. Confidence remains low in more refined details due to\ncontinued disagreement in modeled guidance regarding whether or\nnot sufficient forcing for ascent will spread into our region. \nAt this time, light snow looks unlikely, but cannot be \ncompletely ruled out should forcing for ascent move through \novernight.",
